# SYNOPSIS

```usage
cut -b list [-n] [file ...]
cut -c list [file ...]
cut -f list [-d delim] [-s] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`cut` prints the selected parts of each line of each <file>, or of standard input, as a line.
A <file> of `-` is standard input.

A <list> is numbers and ranges separated by commas or blanks, counted from 1: `N`, `N-M`, `N-` (from <N> to the end) or `-M` (from the start to <M>).
They may come in any order and overlap; each part is printed once, in the order it is in the line.

: `-b` <list>
  Select bytes.
: `-n`
  With `-b`, do not split a UTF-8 character: a range ending inside one ends before it.
: `-c` <list>
  Select characters, which are UTF-8 decoded.
: `-f` <list>
  Select fields, which are separated by the delimiter, a tab unless `-d` says otherwise; the fields printed are joined by it.
  A line with no delimiter in it is printed whole.
: `-d` <delim>
  Separate fields by <delim>, a string, in which backslash escapes such as `\t` are understood.
: `-s`
  With `-f`, leave out lines with no delimiter.

`cut` is sbase's, a hard link to `/boot/bin/posix/sbase-box`; there is no native one.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it as `/bin/cut`.

# EXAMPLES

From the sbase test, `tests/posix/sbasetest.c`, which runs each command as a shell would:

```text
input:   a:b:c
command: cut -d: -f2
output:  b
```

